// @check
// @error 'pair' is not a method — its first parameter is named `a`, not `self`, and a UFCS call `x.pair(…)` dispatches only to a `self` first parameter; rename it to `self`, or call `pair(…)` directly
// UFCS IN VL *IS* THE `self`-FUNCTION RULE, and until v1's defaults work the
// CHECKER was not asking it. `emit_rewrite.drwSelfFnOf` has always required the
// first parameter to be NAMED `self` before rewriting `o.f(a)` into `f(o, a)`,
// while `ufcsCallTy` asked only whether the receiver is assignable to parameter
// 0 — so this program was `vl check`-clean and then `emitProgram: callee is not
// a function name` at build time. Measured identical on the pre-defaults
// compiler, so it is not new; it surfaced because widening UFCS to the arity
// RANGE would otherwise have widened its reach to the defaulted spelling too.
//
// Refusing at the checker is clause 2's second horn, not a relabelling: either
// the program is legal and should compile, or it is illegal and the checker owed
// the diagnosis. `5.pair(2)` is illegal — `pair` is not a method of anything.
//
// D1570 — AND THE SENTENCE NOW SAYS WHY. It read `member access '.pair' on
// non-object i32`, which names the receiver and the property the author wrote and
// nothing about the rule that turned the call down; a reader who has not met the
// `self` rule goes looking for a field on `i32`. The RULE is unchanged — what the
// message adds is the parameter's name and the one-word edit (glean VL-021).
function pair(a: i32, b: i32): i32 { a + b }
print(5.pair(2))
